Z||000005||Jncomutt||07-08-2001||11:23 AM||||Yea yea, that dog thing is over my head, but as far as the bosch platniums +4, I thought I heard to never use them?   Or was this just nonsense??||||reg||1
Z||000006||862M4inCA||07-08-2001||12:21 PM||||As far as plugs I've always used the basic Bosch Platinum plugs and NEVER had a problem. As for the multi-electrode plugs, I tend to stay away from them for one basic reason (which really makes sense) When you have multiple electrodes to a point you ARE blocking the spark at any point where there is an extra electrode. Therefore the fuel mixture can only make it to the spark where there is a gap between the electrodes, and when the plug does fire the initial spark is strong, but due to a lower concentration of fuel mixture surrounding it (because the extra electrodes are in the way)you probably won't see a HUGE increase in milage or power, and therefore in my opinion it's not worth the extra $5+/plug over a basic set of single electrode plugs. Just my $.02    862M4inCA||||reg||1
Z||000007||johnt671||07-08-2001||02:08 PM||||If I remember the thread right, the DIS system on the newer 4 cyl. seems to have a problem with platinum plugs.  I used Rapidfire plugs in mine but didn,t see any inprovement over the plugs that were in it when I got it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fotofrank||07-08-2001||09:17 PM||||I've got an MSD6Aand Blaster coil. Iuse Champion spark plugs(just habit, always used em. No matter what plug you use, with an MSD box use steel or copper plugs. The "platinum ones actually dont work well with that type of aftermarket ignition(MSD,Jackobs Mallory etc. Good luck <P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||14
